Notwithstanding the provisions of this article, in the event that a natural disaster occurs within the City of Americus and is so declared by the governing body of the city, the city shall be authorized, upon approval by the governing body of the city, to purchase landscaping trees, shrubs, bushes, plants, landscaping materials, landscaping services, and similar items, materials, or services (hereinafter referred to as "landscaping renovations") for planting, installation, and use upon publicly owned property damaged by such natural disaster without the necessity for competitive bidding for such landscaping renovation, unless competitive bidding is required in connection with such purchases by federal or state law, under the following circumstances:
(1) Any group of local citizens interested in the landscaping needs of the city, particularly as such needs relate to landscaping damages occurring to publicly owned property of the city damaged by a natural disaster, which takes steps to establish and constitute themselves as an Americus Re-Greening Committee, consisting of either local citizens, members of the governing body, or a combination thereof, may be recognized by the city as a private citizens' committee, but such committee shall be deemed to be solely a private committee and not a committee appointed by the governing body of the city, and neither shall such committee be deemed to be an agency or agent of the city;
(2) The existence of such committee shall be reported in writing to the city manager of the city upon the establishment thereof, and the city manager shall be authorized to provide such assistance, including technical assistance to the committee, as the city manager may deem appropriate once the committee is recognized by the city;
(3) Such committee shall be authorized to investigate, review and assess the damages and determine the required landscaping needs and landscaping renovations, including determining the quantity and type of landscaping trees, shrubs, bushes, plants, landscaping materials, landscaping services, and similar items, materials and services that are necessary in order to repair the damage to the landscaping caused by or resulting from the natural disaster with respect to such publicly owned property within the city;
(4) After such investigation, review and assessment, if the committee desires to provide its findings and recommendations to the city, the committee shall review the requirements of the landscaping renovations that the committee has determined to be necessary with any local and nonlocal vendors that the committee determines have or may have the capability and qualifications to provide such landscaping renovations as the committee may determine appropriate, in order that the committee may determine the best available price, the best material and service availability, and the vendor or vendors which can best meet the requirements found by the committee to be necessary in order to undertake and complete the landscaping renovations;
(5) After such determination, if the committee desires to provide its findings and recommendations to the city, the committee may make written recommendations to the governing body of the city regarding:
a. The required landscaping renovations;
b. The cost thereof;
c. The vendor or vendors that the committee has determined can best meet the requirements found by the committee to be necessary in order to undertake and complete the landscaping renovations; and
d. Any other matters that the committee may deem appropriate.
(6) The governing body shall be authorized to consider such recommendations, and may approve, disapprove, or modify and approve, such recommendations, and if the recommendations are approved or approved with modifications by the governing body, and if the landscaping renovations and purchases are to be made utilizing funds donated or granted to the city by other public agencies, public foundations, private entities, private foundations, or private individuals, the committee may act as agent for the city in procuring such landscaping renovations and purchases which are approved by the governing body, and the governing body shall be authorized to approve and authorize the expenditure of such donated or grant funds for such landscaping renovations and purchases without the necessity of utilizing a competitive bidding process.
(Ord. No. O-07-09-33, § 10-2.1, 9-20-2007; Ord. No. O-2015-15, 7-23-2015)

